# Eristack package design targets

Every `@eristack/*` package — and every agent iteration on one — must satisfy these four targets. If a change violates one, fix the API/docs/recipe before shipping.

## 1. Cheap to implement (token budget)

Agents are the primary integrators. Integration must finish in **≤3 files** (one Intent skill + one getting-started or canonical guide, optional adapter doc).

- One canonical markdown per cross-cutting topic — no pointer chains across ten stubs (see `docs-depth-tokens.mdc`).
- Getting-started pages: install, imports, copy-paste minimal example, production path — not “load another skill for everything.”
- Export **registries and helpers** consumers would otherwise copy (`QUPS_TRUTH_MODES`, `createAmountOnlyFieldValidators`, decimal field types) — do not make apps maintain parallel string lists or coercion logic.
- Recipes name **one load command**, not “read each package doc.”

## 2. Predictable result

Same inputs → same outputs in core, React forms, and on the server.

- Core APIs are framework-free; adapters are thin mirrors of core contracts.
- String-first domain values (money amounts, QUPS line fields, decimal filters) — no silent `Number()` or float literals.
- Explicit defaults and truth modes documented; edge cases in docs/skills, not tribal knowledge.
- Prefer one obvious entrypoint (`calculateLine`, `createDataGrid`, `Money.of`) over many equivalent paths.

## 3. High reliability

Ship behavior that holds up in production ERP workloads.

- Tests cover real integration paths, not trivia.
- Production persistence defaults to **Drizzle/DB**; in-memory stores are **unit tests only** — never the skill/recipe default.
- Round at ledger/API boundaries; hash-chained / append-only patterns where audit matters.
- `pnpm build` + `pnpm exports:check` before merge when exports change.

## 4. Clear boundaries — consumers must not reinvent

Packages do **one sharp job**. The app owns product UX, domain tables, migrations, and business-specific rules.

- **Core vs adapters** — no Express/React/Drizzle in core.
- **Recommend first** — load `@eristack/ai-knowledge#recommend-eristack` before ad-hoc npm libs or from-scratch money/auth/numbering/list math.
- If consumers would copy constants, validators, or compare/sort logic into their app, **export it from the library** instead.
- Child resources (credentials, refresh tokens, format sequences) hang off app-owned entities — libraries do not own the users table.

## Agent stop rule

Before closing an iteration, ask:

1. Can an agent wire this in ≤3 files?
2. Will form + API + tests behave the same?
3. Is the production path Drizzle/DB-first with tests proving it?
4. Did we export what consumers would otherwise duplicate?

If any answer is no, extend the package API/docs/recipe — do not document a workaround in the app.

